   245	## 4. Local lengths on the good open set are the \(\Lambda(F)\)'s
   246	
   247	Delete from \(\mathbf A^1_f\) the finitely many values of the
   248	\(p\)-vertical boundary components. Shrink once more to avoid the critical
   249	values of the restrictions of \(p\) to horizontal boundary components and
   250	the finitely many boundary crossings. Call the resulting nonempty open set
   251	\(U\).
   252	
   253	For \(a\in U\):
   254	
   255	- the divisor \(p^*[a]\) has no boundary-only component and is the strict
   256	  compactified affine fiber \(C_a\);
   257	- near \(p^{-1}(a)\), the full pole divisor \(Q_\infty\) equals \(H\);
   258	- after normalization \(\nu_a:\overline R_a\to C_a\), the pullback of
   259	  \(Q_\infty\) is the polar divisor of the meromorphic function \(g\).
   260	
   261	Thus, at the normalization branch corresponding to a puncture \(P\),
   262	
   263	\[
   264	i_P^{\mathrm{br}}(C_a,H)
   265	:=\operatorname{ord}_P(\nu_a^*H)
   266	=\operatorname{ord}_P(g)_\infty
   267	=\Lambda(P).
   268	\tag{6}
   269	\]
   270	
   271	If \(F\in T_{a,\mathrm{pole}}\), let \(Z_F\) be the cluster of punctures
   272	represented by \(F\). Proposition 5.6, formula (19), says exactly that the
   273	local intersection length of this cluster is
   274	
   275	\[
   276	i_{Z_F}(C_a,H)
   277	=\sum_{P\in Z_F}i_P^{\mathrm{br}}(C_a,H)
   278	=\sum_{P\in Z_F}\Lambda(P)
   279	=\frac{D_{g,F}\deg p_F}{\nu_F}
   280	=\Lambda(F).
   281	\tag{7}
   282	\]
   283	
   284	Summing (7) and using the flat rank (5) proves (P5.8) for every \(a\in U\).
   285	This is the generic-fiber reconstruction recorded in
   286	`SIGRAY-AUDIT.md:74`.
