xmodel/sol-prob.log:18355:+**Estimate: 65%.** I interpret FC3 as the intended universal closure claim behind the td-11 refile: every genuine Q+E5/E5F realization is represented by a finite, completeness-preserving typed refile and is eliminated by a named instrument. “Another load-bearing dependency” means a mathematical claim actually consumed by the current promoted record—such as FC1-R, the corrected Sigray trust subset, GGV22 Proposition 4.3, or exact certificate provenance—not an openly conjectural avenue such as TD-BOUND/G5, a known retired claim, or the mere absence of a proof. I put FC3 alone at about 45%. It remains explicitly UNKNOWN and “the wall”; its cap-free compiler still needs several completeness and surjectivity obligations. The prior refile/census breakages, the td-11 expansion from 159 to 411 rows after an omitted realized chart, the refutation of the proposed beyond-core bound, and the stalled FC1 exploration make a substantial miss probability rational. The wider union risk is raised by the Sigray audit’s nine new errata and seven gaps among 72 audited items, incomplete auditing of later consumed sections, and the pinned unrefereed GGV22 dependency. Against this, the narrow promoted claims are carefully scoped and often have exact replay, independent cross-checks, and hostile review, so I would not put the union near certainty. See `TOWER-TD11.md` §18, `xmodel/sol-td11-13-scope.md`, `SIGRAY-AUDIT.md`, and `REDUCTION.md`.
